# Dancing raisins: how much gas does it take to lift one?
#
# A raisin is heavier than water, so it sinks. Bubbles stick to its
# wrinkled skin, and when enough of them have gathered the raisin and its
# bubbles TOGETHER are lighter than the water — up it goes. At the top the
# bubbles pop and it comes back down. Nothing reacts in this experiment:
# the raisin is the same raisin all the way through. What changes is only
# where it is.
register lv2

# 1. The control. A raisin in plain water, with no gas anywhere. It sinks,
#    and it stays there, and the bench says nothing about dancing because
#    there is nothing to dance on.
add v1 raisin 5g
add v1 water 200mL
look v1

# 2. The experiment. Put the raisin in first and pour the fizzy water over
#    it — which is also how you do it in a kitchen. Read the number: the
#    bubbles have to be worth about a third of the raisin's own size.
new beaker
add v2 raisin 5g
add v2 sparkling_water 200mL
look v2

# 3. Now make the liquid heavier. Sugar syrup is denser than water, so the
#    raisin is much closer to floating already — and the gas has far less
#    work to do. Same raisin, same bubbles, a third of the lift needed.
new beaker
add v3 raisin 5g
add v3 water 120mL
add v3 sucrose 200g
add v3 sparkling_water 100mL
look v3

# What this does not model: how long the dancing lasts, how many trips the
# raisin makes, how big the bubbles are or how many stick. This bench lets
# a glass of fizzy water go flat in a single step, so there is no clock to
# put a period on. What it computes is the density of the object, the
# density of the liquid it is in, and how much attached gas would lift it —
# and that last number is what the experiment is actually about.
